---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import TreeFig from '../../components/TreeFig.astro';
import BstLab from '../../components/BstLab.astro';
import Quiz from '../../components/Quiz.astro';
import CodeWalk from '../../components/CodeWalk.astro';
import Peek from '../../components/Peek.astro';
import TreeBuilder from '../../components/TreeBuilder.astro';
import { idInsert, treeNodePic } from '../../lib/illus.js';
import { insertCode, insertSteps, loopCode, loopSteps, insertFigs, searchCode, treeNodeCode } from '../../lib/walks.js';
import { course } from '../../data/itc120.js';
import { hi, hiN } from '../../lib/code.js';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const start = [40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70];
const figs = insertFigs();
const searchPeek = `<pre class="code"><code>${hiN(searchCode)}</code></pre>`;
const bugCode = String.raw`} else if (value < current.data) {
  if (current.left == null) {
    current = new TreeNode(value);
    return root;
  }
  current = current.left;
}`;
const prac = [50, 30, 75, 25, 40, 35, 90, 80, 95];

const quiz = [
  { q: 'Where does a new value go in a BST?', opts: ['Always at the root', 'At the empty spot (null) where a search for it falls off the tree', 'Always as the left child of the root', 'At the end, like an array'], a: 1, why: 'Insert = <b>search</b> for the value, then attach it at the null spot. It always becomes a new <b>leaf</b>.' },
  { q: '<code>current</code> refers to node 50, and 50\'s left is empty. Which line connects 45?', opts: ['<code>current = new TreeNode(45);</code>', '<code>current.left = new TreeNode(45);</code>', '<code>current = current.left;</code>', '<code>root = new TreeNode(45);</code>'], a: 1, why: '<code>current.left = ...</code> changes the tree. <code>current = ...</code> only moves the arrow, so 45 would never be connected.' },
  { q: 'After <code>current.left = new TreeNode(45);</code>, what does <code>current</code> refer to?', opts: ['45', '50 (it did not move)', 'null', 'The root'], a: 1, why: 'This line connects a new node but does <b>not</b> move <code>current</code>.' },
  { q: 'What does <code>insert(root, 30)</code> do if 30 is already in the tree?', opts: ['Adds a second 30', 'Nothing: it returns the same root', 'Replaces the root with 30', 'Throws an error'], a: 1, why: 'Lines 8–9: equal → <code>return root;</code>. Our BSTs have no duplicates, so the tree stays the same.' },
  { q: 'Why do we write <code>root = insert(root, value);</code>?', opts: ['To make the new value the root', 'Because when the tree is empty, insert returns a new root that must be saved', 'Java requires it for every method', 'To print the tree'], a: 1, why: 'Usually insert returns the same root. But for an <b>empty</b> tree, it returns the new node; without <code>root =</code>, that node is lost.' },
  { q: 'Does <code>root = insert(root, 45);</code> make 45 the root?', opts: ['Yes', 'No: 45 is added at the bottom, and insert gives back the same top node', 'Only if 45 is the largest', 'Only in an AVL tree'], a: 1, why: 'The method returns the same top node it received (unless the tree was empty). <code>root =</code> just saves it again.' },
];
---
<Base title="BST Insertion · ITC 120" description="Inserting into a binary search tree, by hand and with a Java loop: move current, connect a new node." course={{ code: course.code, href: base + course.href }} toc>
  <header class="wrap lesson-hero flow">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href={base + course.href}>ITC 120</a> › Midterm › Trees and BSTs</p>
    <p class="kicker">Trees and BSTs · Lesson 3 of 6</p>
    <h1>BST Insertion</h1>
    <p class="lede">Adding a value by hand, then in Java: search for the value, and where the search falls off the tree, attach the new node.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">In this lesson, you will:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>insert into a BST <b>by hand</b>, using the same questions as search;</li>
        <li>learn the two actions in the code: <b>move</b> <code>current</code> and <b>connect</b> a new node;</li>
        <li>trace <code>insert</code>, including a value that is already there and an empty tree;</li>
        <li>build a whole BST with a loop, and avoid three common mistakes.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> the <a href={base + '/itc120/bst/'}>BST lesson</a>, and scratch paper.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="hand">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · By hand</p>
      <h2>Insertion by hand</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>A new student enrolls, and their ID must be added to the tree in a place where search will find it later. Putting it anywhere would break the BST rule. So: <span class="w-key">search for the value; where the search reaches null, attach the new node</span>.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus">
        <Fragment set:html={idInsert()} />
        <figcaption>The registrar program does not look for "any free spot." It asks the same questions as a search, and the first empty spot it reaches is the only correct place.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>Insert asks the same questions as <Peek label="the search method" title="From the BST lesson" html={searchPeek} />. Only the endings are different, as this table shows:</p>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Search</th><th>Insert</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Tree empty?</td><td>return false</td><td>the new node becomes the root</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Equal?</td><td>found</td><td>already there: skip</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Smaller?</td><td><span class="w-left">go left</span></td><td>left empty? <b>attach there</b>. If not, <span class="w-left">go left</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Larger?</td><td><span class="w-right">go right</span></td><td>right empty? <b>attach there</b>. If not, <span class="w-right">go right</span></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Put together, inserting by hand follows a short checklist. It is the search checklist with new endings:</p>
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Insert checklist</p>
        <p>Empty tree? The new node becomes the root. Otherwise, start at the root and compare the new value with the current node:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>equal?</b> → already in the tree. Skip it. Stop.</li>
          <li><b>smaller?</b> → look <span class="w-left">left</span>. Empty? Attach it there and stop. Not empty? Go left, back to 1.</li>
          <li><b>larger?</b> → look <span class="w-right">right</span>. Empty? Attach it there and stop. Not empty? Go right, back to 1.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The new value always ends up as a new <b>leaf</b>. No other node moves.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Try it in the lab below. Each example shows the walk down, one check at a time. The chip at the top left of the tree says what <b>this</b> step did; the tree shows where we are now.</p>
      <BstLab id="lab-insert" mode="insert" cap="Insert lab" hint="Watch the “current” label. The tree only changes at the very last step. Then insert your own numbers: each one is added to the same tree." legend={`<span><i class="l-cur2"></i> current</span><span><i class="l-path"></i> checked</span><span><i class="l-new"></i> new node</span>`} presets={[
        { label: 'Insert 45', values: start, op: 45 },
        { label: 'Insert 30 (already there)', values: start, op: 30 },
        { label: 'Empty tree, insert 7', values: [], op: 7 },
      ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="code">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · Reading the code</p>
      <h2>The insert method</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Here is one way to write insert in Java. It uses a loop, like the search method, and builds new nodes with <Peek label="the TreeNode class" title="From the Trees lesson" html={`<pre class="code"><code>${hi(treeNodeCode)}</code></pre>` + treeNodePic()} />. Before reading it line by line, meet the names it uses. This table lists each one:</p>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>root</code></td><td>refers to the top node; <code>null</code> when the tree is empty</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>value</code></td><td>the number we want to insert; it stays the same during the call</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>current</code></td><td>refers to the node we are checking now; it moves down the tree</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>current.data</code></td><td>the number stored in that node</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>current.left</code>, <code>current.right</code></td><td>that node's children (either can be <code>null</code>)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>The two actions</h3>
        <p>To understand the code, learn these two actions first:</p>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div class="paper flow">
            <p class="note-label">1. Move current (line 15 or 21)</p>
            <p class="mono">current = current.right;</p>
            <Fragment set:html={figs.move} />
            <p><code>current</code> moves to a child that already exists. <b>The tree does not change</b>. Think of <code>current</code> as a finger pointing at one node: moving the finger does not change the tree.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="paper flow">
            <p class="note-label">2. Connect a new node (line 12 or 18)</p>
            <p class="mono">current.left = new TreeNode(45);</p>
            <Fragment set:html={figs.connect} />
            <p>A new node is connected through the empty left (or right) of the node <code>current</code> refers to. <b>The tree changes</b>, but <code>current</code> does not move.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="remember"><p class="note-label">Compare</p><p><code>current = ...</code> moves the arrow. <code>current.left = ...</code> changes the tree.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Step through the code</h3>
        <p>Now watch the whole method insert 45 into the starting tree. The dashed boxes are the empty (<code>null</code>) spots; the yellow one is where 45 will go.</p>
        <CodeWalk id="walk-insert" code={insertCode} steps={insertSteps([40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70], 45)} cap="insert(root, 45), step by step" />
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>What each part does</h3>
        <p>Here is the same method as a short summary, part by part:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Lines 3–5:</b> empty tree? The new node is the root.</li>
          <li><b>Line 6:</b> start at the root, like search.</li>
          <li><b>Lines 8–9:</b> equal? The value is already there. Stop.</li>
          <li><b>Lines 11–14, 17–20:</b> empty spot on that side? <b>Connect</b> the new node there, then stop.</li>
          <li><b>Lines 15, 21:</b> not empty? <b>Move</b> to that child, then check again.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><code>return</code> exits the <b>whole method</b> right away, not just the <code>if</code> or the loop.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="calling">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · Calling insert</p>
      <h2><code>root = insert(root, 45);</code></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Java first runs <code>insert(root, 45)</code>: it adds 45, then gives back the top of the tree. Then <code>root =</code> saves what it gave back.</p>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Does 45 become the root?</p>
        <p><b>No!</b> 45 is added at the bottom (as 50's left child, as you saw in <Peek label="the last step of the walkthrough" title="Connecting 45 (10 and 30 not shown)" html={figs.connect} />). The method gives back the same top node it was given (40), and <code>root =</code> just saves that same node again.</p>
      </div>
      <p><b>Then why save it?</b> When the tree is <b>empty</b>, the new node becomes the top. The method gives back a <i>new</i> top, and we must save it. Try "Empty tree, insert 7" in the lab.</p>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Build a whole tree with a loop</h3>
        <p>To build a whole tree, call insert once for each value. Step through the loop and watch the first pass: that is the empty-tree case, where <code>root =</code> really matters.</p>
        <CodeWalk id="walk-loop" code={loopCode} steps={loopSteps(start)} cap="Building a BST with a loop" hint="Use Next to go through the loop one pass at a time, or tap a line." />
        <p><span class="w-key">The order of insertion decides the shape.</span> Here are the same four values, inserted in two different orders:</p>
        <div class="two-col">
          <figure class="fig"><p class="fig-cap">Order: 30, 10, 40, 20</p><TreeFig values={[30, 10, 40, 20]} label="BST from 30, 10, 40, 20: 30 at the root, 10 and 40 below, 20 under 10" /><p class="small">Short and wide: height 2.</p></figure>
          <figure class="fig"><p class="fig-cap">Order: 10, 20, 30, 40 (sorted)</p><TreeFig values={[10, 20, 30, 40]} label="BST from 10, 20, 30, 40: a chain going right" /><p class="small">A chain: height 3. Search becomes as slow as a linked list.</p></figure>
        </div>
        <p>Inserting 10, 20, 30, … 70 in sorted order would make an even longer chain. Fixing this is the job of AVL trees, later in the course.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="mistakes">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · Watch out</p>
      <h2>Three common mistakes</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>These three mistakes are the most common ones in student code. Each one compiles and runs, but the tree comes out wrong:</p>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">1. Forgetting <code>root =</code></p>
        <p><code>insert(root, 25);</code> on an empty tree creates node 25 and returns it, but nothing saves it. <code>root</code> is still <code>null</code>. Fix: always write <code>root = insert(root, 25);</code></p>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">2. Moving instead of connecting</p>
        <p><code>current = new TreeNode(45);</code> only moves the arrow. 50's left is still <code>null</code>, so 45 is never connected. Fix: <code>current.left = new TreeNode(45);</code></p>
        <Fragment set:html={figs.lost45} />
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">3. Connecting without checking for null</p>
        <p>Insert 25: at 40, 25 &lt; 40, so we look left. If the code writes <code>current.left = new TreeNode(25);</code> without checking, it <b>replaces</b> 20, and 20, 10, and 30 are cut off from the tree. That is why the code checks <code>if (current.left == null)</code> first.</p>
        <Fragment set:html={figs.cut} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="practice">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 5 · Your turn</p>
      <h2>Try it on paper</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Problem 1: trace table</p>
        <p>Using <Peek label="the starting BST" title="The starting BST" html={figs.startTree} /> (40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70), make a trace table for <code>insert(root, 55)</code>: one row each time the loop checks a node.</p>
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show the answer</summary><div>
          <table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>current.data</th><th>Compare</th><th>Child on that side</th><th>Then</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>40</td><td>55 &gt; 40</td><td>right = 60</td><td>move right</td></tr>
              <tr><td>60</td><td>55 &lt; 60</td><td>left = 50</td><td>move left</td></tr>
              <tr><td>50</td><td>55 &gt; 50</td><td>right = null</td><td>connect 55; return root</td></tr>
            </tbody></table>
        </div></details>
      </div>
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Problem 2: build and answer</p>
        <p>Start with an empty tree. Insert, in this order: <span class="mono">{prac.join(', ')}</span></p>
        <p>Draw it on paper first. Then build it below, placing the values in the same order, and tap Check.</p>
        <TreeBuilder id="build-prac" values={prac} target="50(30(25,40(35,)),75(,90(80,95)))" cap="Build the final BST" />
        <ol><li>Draw the final BST.</li><li>Which node is the parent of 35?</li><li>If we then call <code>insert(root, 85)</code>, which nodes does <code>current</code> visit? Where is 85 connected?</li><li>What happens if we call <code>insert(root, 40)</code>?</li></ol>
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show the answer</summary><div class="flow">
          <TreeFig values={prac} label="BST built from 50, 30, 75, 25, 40, 35, 90, 80, 95" />
          <ol>
            <li>See the drawing.</li>
            <li>40 (35 &lt; 50: left. 35 &gt; 30: right. 35 &lt; 40: left. Empty, so 35 is connected there.)</li>
            <li>50, 75, 90, 80. 85 &gt; 80 and 80's right is empty, so 85 becomes the right child of 80.</li>
            <li>Nothing changes. current visits 50, 30, 40; 40 is equal, so it is skipped.</li>
          </ol>
        </div></details>
      </div>
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Problem 3: find the bug</p>
        <p>A classmate inserted 45, but 45 never appears in the tree. Which line is wrong?</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(bugCode)} /></pre>
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show the answer</summary><div>
          <p>Line 3: <code>current = new TreeNode(value);</code> only moves <code>current</code>; the new node is never connected. It should be <code>current.left = new TreeNode(value);</code></p>
        </div></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="check">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 6 · Check yourself</p>
      <h2>Self-check</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Not graded. Tap an answer to see if you got it, and read why.</p>
      <Quiz id="quiz-ins" questions={quiz} />
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Key takeaways</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Insert = search for the value, then attach it at the null spot. It becomes a new leaf.</li>
          <li><code>current = current.left;</code> moves; <code>current.left = new TreeNode(v);</code> connects.</li>
          <li>Always check for null before connecting, or you cover up a node.</li>
          <li>Always write <code>root = insert(root, value);</code></li>
          <li>Insertion order decides the shape of the tree.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <p><b>Next lesson:</b> <a href={base + '/itc120/traversals/'}>Tree traversals</a>. (In Lesson 5, you will also see a recursive version of insert.)</p>
      <div class="refs small muted">
        <p class="note-label">References</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Programiz, "Binary Search Tree (BST)." <a href="https://www.programiz.com/dsa/binary-search-tree">programiz.com/dsa/binary-search-tree</a></li>
          <li>Oracle, "TreeSet (Java SE 21 &amp; JDK 21)," Java API documentation.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .refs .note-label { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--step-0); margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
  .refs ol { display: grid; gap: 0.3rem; }
</style>
